1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:03,522
A search retrieves four matches: passage

2
00:00:03,542 --> 00:00:06,641
two from paper A, passage seven from paper

3
00:00:06,661 --> 00:00:10,146
A, passage three from paper B, and passage

4
00:00:10,166 --> 00:00:11,473
nine from paper A.

5
00:00:11,635 --> 00:00:13,277
How many papers did it find?

6
00:00:13,504 --> 00:00:15,545
Four matches, two papers.

7
00:00:15,720 --> 00:00:17,968
Would the interface show four results, or

8
00:00:17,988 --> 00:00:19,785
two? Make a prediction.

9
00:00:22,200 --> 00:00:23,938
Either display is possible.

10
00:00:24,101 --> 00:00:26,800
Matching passages may appear separately or

11
00:00:26,820 --> 00:00:28,913
be grouped by source identity.

12
00:00:29,075 --> 00:00:31,439
The visible result list hides earlier

13
00:00:31,459 --> 00:00:33,759
decisions about what was indexed and

14
00:00:33,779 --> 00:00:36,658
represented. Those decisions determine

15
00:00:36,704 --> 00:00:38,836
which evidence can reach the list in the

16
00:00:38,856 --> 00:00:40,008
first place.

17
00:00:40,912 --> 00:00:43,104
Take the chapter’s illustrative twenty

18
00:00:43,124 --> 00:00:46,068
page article. A system might index one

19
00:00:46,088 --> 00:00:49,586
abstract, ten sections, or fifty

20
00:00:49,606 --> 00:00:51,196
overlapping passages.

21
00:00:51,358 --> 00:00:53,580
These are alternative choices, not

22
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:55,590
measurements of a named service.

23
00:00:55,752 --> 00:00:57,807
They produce different searchable objects

24
00:00:57,827 --> 00:00:59,443
from the same source document.

25
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:02,363
An abstract is a compact account.

26
00:01:02,525 --> 00:01:05,339
A section can preserve more local context.

27
00:01:05,502 --> 00:01:08,330
A short overlapping window may expose a

28
00:01:08,350 --> 00:01:09,708
narrow passage.

29
00:01:09,870 --> 00:01:11,977
The right unit depends on the question and

30
00:01:11,997 --> 00:01:12,930
pipeline.

31
00:01:13,854 --> 00:01:16,725
In a conventional dense bi encoder, each

32
00:01:16,745 --> 00:01:19,148
indexed passage receives one pooled

33
00:01:19,168 --> 00:01:22,434
vector. Fifty passages therefore become

34
00:01:22,454 --> 00:01:25,513
fifty independently searchable vectors.

35
00:01:25,688 --> 00:01:28,245
No single one automatically represents the

36
00:01:28,265 --> 00:01:29,482
complete paper.

37
00:01:30,026 --> 00:01:32,599
Nothing automatically combines evidence

38
00:01:32,619 --> 00:01:35,575
spread across those fifty chunks.

39
00:01:35,724 --> 00:01:37,560
A passage might describe the study

40
00:01:37,580 --> 00:01:39,635
population while another reports the

41
00:01:39,655 --> 00:01:42,560
outcome. Finding one passage does not

42
00:01:42,580 --> 00:01:45,112
prove that both facts were compared

43
00:01:45,132 --> 00:01:47,135
jointly when the retrieval score was

44
00:01:47,155 --> 00:01:48,114
calculated.

45
00:01:49,020 --> 00:01:51,338
Here is a declared illustration.

46
00:01:51,513 --> 00:01:53,374
One chunk says the participants were

47
00:01:53,394 --> 00:01:55,913
adults. Another contains a numerical

48
00:01:55,933 --> 00:01:58,439
outcome, while a neighbouring paragraph

49
00:01:58,459 --> 00:02:00,152
explains a limitation.

50
00:02:00,315 --> 00:02:03,117
A question that needs all three facts can

51
00:02:03,137 --> 00:02:05,784
outgrow any one retrieved unit.

52
00:02:06,342 --> 00:02:09,075
Overlapping windows may retain some shared

53
00:02:09,095 --> 00:02:12,400
context, but an overlap is no guarantee

54
00:02:12,420 --> 00:02:15,337
that every decisive relationship fits.

55
00:02:15,487 --> 00:02:18,534
A later stage might gather neighbours,

56
00:02:18,554 --> 00:02:21,446
fetch the parent section, or combine

57
00:02:21,466 --> 00:02:22,322
evidence.

58
00:02:23,213 --> 00:02:25,039
Now hide a claim in the results section

59
00:02:25,059 --> 00:02:26,843
and leave it out of the abstract.

60
00:02:26,979 --> 00:02:29,653
An abstract only index cannot directly

61
00:02:29,673 --> 00:02:32,539
match that claim as indexed text.

62
00:02:32,701 --> 00:02:34,511
It might retrieve the paper for other

63
00:02:34,531 --> 00:02:37,217
evidence, but the missing section was not

64
00:02:37,237 --> 00:02:39,497
available to that retrieval stage.

65
00:02:40,063 --> 00:02:43,676
The chapter contrasts Wiley AI Gateway’s

66
00:02:43,696 --> 00:02:46,318
documented full text chunks with discovery

67
00:02:46,338 --> 00:02:48,818
records described by metadata and

68
00:02:48,838 --> 00:02:49,913
abstracts.

69
00:02:50,812 --> 00:02:53,657
Retrieval is not always the final evidence

70
00:02:53,677 --> 00:02:56,492
boundary. After selecting an abstract or

71
00:02:56,512 --> 00:03:00,241
passage, a system might fetch full text,

72
00:03:00,262 --> 00:03:02,884
expand to a parent section, or add

73
00:03:02,904 --> 00:03:04,262
neighbouring passages.

74
00:03:04,818 --> 00:03:07,541
Ask three separate questions: what text

75
00:03:07,561 --> 00:03:10,144
was indexed, what unit was retrieved, and

76
00:03:10,164 --> 00:03:12,463
what evidence was supplied as context?

77
00:03:12,600 --> 00:03:15,466
A later fetch can supply previously absent

78
00:03:15,486 --> 00:03:17,889
text. Whether that evidence reaches an

79
00:03:17,909 --> 00:03:21,085
answer correctly remains another question

80
00:03:21,105 --> 00:03:21,922
to check.

81
00:03:22,805 --> 00:03:27,184
Return to passages A two, A seven, B three

82
00:03:27,204 --> 00:03:29,993
and A nine. Grouping by source produces

83
00:03:30,013 --> 00:03:33,615
paper A with three matching passages, and

84
00:03:33,635 --> 00:03:34,903
paper B with one.

85
00:03:35,078 --> 00:03:37,738
It does not, by itself, specify how

86
00:03:37,758 --> 00:03:40,135
passage scores become a paper score.

87
00:03:40,683 --> 00:03:42,967
It also does not merge their claims into a

88
00:03:42,987 --> 00:03:44,165
supported answer.

89
00:03:44,327 --> 00:03:46,717
A system needs explicit choices for

90
00:03:46,737 --> 00:03:49,268
aggregation and any later reasoning.

91
00:03:49,469 --> 00:03:51,846
Four returned chunks are not necessarily

92
00:03:51,866 --> 00:03:54,539
four distinct papers, and two displayed

93
00:03:54,559 --> 00:03:56,756
papers do not reveal how many chunks

94
00:03:56,776 --> 00:03:57,567
competed.

95
00:03:58,486 --> 00:04:00,860
Write down four choices: the source

96
00:04:00,880 --> 00:04:03,154
document, the indexed unit, the

97
00:04:03,174 --> 00:04:05,370
representation of each unit, and the way

98
00:04:05,390 --> 00:04:07,612
matches become displayed results.

99
00:04:07,761 --> 00:04:10,937
Then record any later context expansion.

100
00:04:11,502 --> 00:04:14,122
Ask what text was embedded, how large the

101
00:04:14,142 --> 00:04:16,751
units were, and how matching passages were

102
00:04:16,771 --> 00:04:18,374
combined at article level.

103
00:04:18,536 --> 00:04:20,733
The next chapter asks what a system can

104
00:04:20,753 --> 00:04:23,039
afford after a fast first stage has

105
00:04:23,059 --> 00:04:25,462
produced a shortlist: more careful

106
00:04:25,482 --> 00:04:28,168
comparison, with a new candidate boundary.
